V3DDriver 0.12 rev c - test pack 2 (big screens: is it correct, and is it faster?)
===================================================================================

Thank you for the first round. It showed the GPU driver working correctly on a
3440x1440 screen (VerifySuite: all 450 cases identical to the CPU, including the
cases that cut a big rectangle into several GPU jobs). Two questions are left:

  1. Is it ALSO correct at 32 bits per pixel, and at a true 4K (3840x2160)
     screen if you can set one? (Only 16 bits per pixel at 3440x1440 so far.)
  2. Is it actually FASTER than the CPU at these sizes? The desktop mostly draws
     small things that the driver leaves to the CPU on purpose, so you may not
     notice it; this pack measures it directly.

The folder
----------
  V3DDriver       the module (same one as before: 0.12 rev c)
  VerifySuite     pixel-by-pixel check of the GPU against the CPU
  GpuCheck        prints what the GPU has done so far
  FillCopyBench   NEW: times fills, copies and scrolls of 17 sizes, three ways:
                  CPU only, GPU for everything, and the driver's default rules
  TraceDump       only if something looks wrong

Always double-click scripts in the Filer. NEVER type them into a Task Window
(it swallows the drawing, and the numbers come out meaningless).

Part A - correctness at 32 bits per pixel (about 10 minutes)
------------------------------------------------------------
 1. Set your biggest mode (3840x2160 if you can; otherwise 3440x1440) at
    "16 million colours" (32 bits per pixel). Write down the mode.
 2. Double-click V3DDriver (skip if it is already loaded).
 3. Double-click VerifySuite, press 1. Note the "Screen:" line near the top and
    the last line ("ALL ... IDENTICAL TO THE CPU" is a pass).
 4. Run it again and press 5 (forces many more cuts). Note the last line.

Part B - speed (about 5 minutes per run)
----------------------------------------
 5. Double-click FillCopyBench. It prints the screen depth and the driver's
    limits, then runs three passes (CPU, GPU, GPU with default limits). It
    scribbles on the lower-left of the screen; that is harmless. Do not touch
    the mouse while it runs.
 6. When it says "Done", press SPACE. It prints three tables (fill, copy,
    scroll). PHOTOGRAPH ALL OF THEM. The columns that matter are "Speedup"
    (above 1.00x the GPU is faster) and "Dflt" (what you really get).
 7. Repeat steps 1 to 6 at "65 thousand colours" (16 bits per pixel).

Part C - how it feels (optional)
--------------------------------
 8. Drag a big window around, scroll a long page, switch between large windows.
    Then in a Task Window type   *RMKill V3DDriver   and do the same without the
    GPU. Say honestly whether you can tell the difference.

What to send back
-----------------
  - the exact modes tested (width x height, bits per pixel)
  - the last lines of every VerifySuite run
  - photos of the FillCopyBench tables for each depth
  - anything that glitched or froze (and the last thing on screen)
  - if you have RISCOSmark, its totals with and without the module

Big numbers above 1.00x on the large sizes at the bottom of the tables are the
interesting ones. A result that says the GPU is NOT faster is just as useful.
